From 0cd1ec00610bae5aca878099359e11ef2d4e8e5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 05yuki <ceu.azul.samu@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:20:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] cpu-gpu-overlap: the shared expert forks after the CPU
 request, one kernel stages the hand-off

Contributed by howl9003 in 05yuki/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017-ktransformers#5.

KT_SHARED_AFTER_SUBMIT=1: DeepseekV2MoE.forward_normal_dual_stream forks
the shared expert's GEMMs from an event KTEPWrapperMethod.submit records
right after the CPU request, instead of before the router, so they stop
contending with the router -> top-k -> hand-off chain that feeds the CPU.
Scheduling only.

KT_HANDOFF_PACK=1: submit_forward stages the MoE input for the CPU with one
Triton kernel that writes the hidden rows (bf16), the routing weights
(fp32) and the expert ids (widened to int64) straight into kt-kernel's
pinned host buffers through their device-mapped addresses, in place of the
ids cast and the three DtoH copies the graph serialised before the request
flag. Decode and verify batches only (256 rows at most); a buffer that is
not device-mapped turns the pack off for the process, with a warning.
patches/kt-kernel-cpu-handoff.patch gives submit_forward the packer
argument; a kt-kernel without it keeps the copies.

Numerics: bit-identical. The 24-piece NLL with the hot slots held fixed is
0.7139 with both switches off and on, 11,496 of 11,496 tokens identical
(two RTX 5070 Ti, TP 2); on one SM89 GPU all four switch combinations gave
bit-identical selected-token and top-8 logprobs over 112 cases.

Effect (two RTX 5070 Ti, TP 2, two launches each way): Japanese prose
decode 46.1 / 45.5 -> 48.3 / 47.5 tok/s, the six other texts +2-7%, the
35K prefill unchanged.

Prerequisites: `loading`, `placement`, `prefill-lend`, `stream-prefill`.

